{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>But the midwives feared, etc.</b> One must examine why he repeated, saying <b>and they did not do, and they kept alive.</b> And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sotah 11a) said that they would supply water and food. And one must examine why they did not do so for them beforehand. Perhaps the verse informs us that they did not cease from their kindness, and this is the cause of the fear that they feared את HaElohim — even concerning the withholding of the kindness they were accustomed to perform by supplying food; and not only did they not do, etc., but <b>and they kept alive.</b>" "<b>Or</b> perhaps beforehand they had not given thought to this mitzvah of supplying water and food, and after Pharaoh commanded them, they feared lest they be suspected if one of the newborn died — that they had killed him. Therefore, to clear themselves of suspicion, they strove to keep alive even one who would die of his own accord from lack of sustenance. And perhaps this is what the word <b>et HaElohim</b> comes to include — to cover the obligation of clearing themselves of the suspicion of men, so as to be clean from HaShem and from Israel." "And the reason for his saying <b>as he spoke</b> — the kaph of comparison — is to say: neither he nor anything like him. Or it may intend to say: for it is the way of things that when a king decrees something, at least at the outset of the decree the king's word is strong and the king's awe is overwhelming, so as not to transgress it; and with the passage of time, after the decree, it weakens. And therefore the verse informs us that these righteous women nullified the king's decree close to the time of the decree — and that is what it says, <b>and they did not do</b>; and when? <b>As he spoke</b> — as you would say that at the time they left there and were acting as midwives for the Hebrew women, they did not do, etc.; and moreover they supplied food for no proper reason, and nullified his words close to the decree. And the parsing of <b>as he spoke</b> is like the parsing of (Genesis 27:30): ״כַּאֲשֶׁר כִּלָּה יִצְחָק לְבָרֵךְ״."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:21.482461408Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}